title: A member that restates its note
pid: 28764d3e-2b4d-4435-80e0-096ba233d48a
body: |2-
  A member of the forks folder often says what its note says: a fresh
  clone's original, before you edit the clone, or a note forked to file
  a reply under. The clients say so, mostly in the member's title.
  Where its title and body both equal its note's, they draw
  =<b unchanged>= over its title and hide the lines under it; where only
  its title does, =<b's title unchanged>= over the title; where only its
  body does, =<b's body unchanged>= over the body, its title shown. They
  draw these in the message style (black on white). b is the note, as
  the heralds name it from the member (its grandparent, past the
  folder). Two empty bodies are equal; a body the view omits (a
  write-protected node's, herald B) equals none, being unseen. This is
  drawing only: the buffer holds the member's real title and body, and a
  save sends them as they are.

  Each view buffer has its own switch, on by default: =C-c v u=
  ('skg-toggle-fork-dedup-overlay', the buffer-local variable
  'skg-fork-dedup-overlay'; in Neovim =<localleader>vu=,
  =:SkgToggleForkDedupOverlay=, the buffer variable
  'skg_fork_dedup_overlay') shows the text, to read or edit it. An edit
  that makes the member differ from its note uncovers it. skg-for-ai,
  whose files have no overlays, marks such a member's handle 'title=b',
  'body=b' or both, and leaves a restated body out of its compact file
  ('tools/skg-for-ai/README.org', "Restated titles and bodies").
